<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 4</h2>
<p style="text-align: center;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Weather Report</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How Will the Weather Change Tomorrow?</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Respond to peer writing.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Writing: Can write instructions for using a weather app (e.g., 'Open the app and check the temperature.').</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can list items to bring, based on weather predictions (e.g., 'Bring sunscreen and water if it's sunny.').</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can respond to peer comments on trip plans (e.g., 'It is a good idea to bring umbrellas in case it rains.').</li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a picture of different weather conditions (e.g., sunny beach, rainy street, snowy mountain). Students silently observe the images. The teacher asks students to quickly write down one word to describe each picture (e.g., 'sunny,' 'rainy,' 'cold'). This activates vocabulary from previous lessons and sets the stage for written tasks.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes a simple weather prediction on the board: "Tomorrow, it will be windy in the morning." The teacher models forming simple predictions using 'will be' and relevant vocabulary. Then, the teacher models writing a simple instruction based on a prediction: "If it will be windy, you should bring a jacket." The teacher emphasizes correct sentence structure and word order for writing.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I write 'It will be sunny tomorrow,' what does 'will be' tell us about the time?" (Expected answer: "Future" or "Tomorrow").</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What should I bring if it looks like rain today?" (Expected answer: "An umbrella").</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a short written "Weather Forecast for a School Trip" on a handout or projected slide. The text includes specific predictions for a trip (e.g., "On Saturday morning, it will be sunny with a temperature of 25 degrees. In the afternoon, clouds will appear, and it might rain over the park. You should bring sunscreen and a hat for the morning, and an umbrella for the afternoon."). It explicitly uses future simple, prepositions of place, and modal verbs for recommendations.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students describe weather conditions and use prepositions of place in written context, ask about the forecast through reading, and use modal verbs for recommendations in written form.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the "Weather Forecast" and complete written tasks:
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> Students write short answers to questions like, "What is the main topic of this text?" and "Is the weather good all day?"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read statements about the forecast and write 'True' or 'False' next to each (e.g., "It will be rainy all morning. (False)").</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match weather predictions to recommended items to bring by drawing lines or writing pairs (e.g., "Sunny morning" -> "sunscreen and hat").</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice writing sentences using the future simple tense for weather predictions, prepositions of place, and modal verbs for recommendations. Target vocabulary includes: <i>will be, predict, look like, forecast, bring, sunny, cloudy, warm, cold, rainy, windy, on, at, in, temperature, storm, day, morning, afternoon, evening, sky</i>.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion):</b> The teacher provides sentences with blanks related to weather predictions and recommendations. Students fill in the blanks using target vocabulary and grammar. For example: "Tomorrow, it _______ (be/sunny) in the south." "If it _______ (look like/rain), you _______ (should/bring) an umbrella." Students focus on correct spelling and grammar (e.g., segmenting 'ther-mo-stat' if used).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice (Instruction Writing):</b> In pairs, students receive simple icons or short phrases representing weather conditions and items (e.g., 'sun icon -> sunscreen', 'cloud icon + rain -> umbrella'). They must write short instructions or recommendations based on these prompts. For example, for 'sun icon', they write: "If it will be sunny, you should bring sunscreen." or "Bring sunscreen if it's sunny." They also practice writing instructions for using a weather app, such as 'Open the app and check the temperature.'</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Check (Sentence Correction):</b> Students exchange their written instructions and recommendations from Activity 2 with a different pair. Each pair reads the other's work and silently identifies one grammatical error or spelling mistake. They gently circle it without correcting it and return it. This encourages attention to accuracy in writing.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Written Response to Peer Travel Plans.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activity:</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group creates a simple written "Trip Plan" for a short imaginary outdoor activity (e.g., "A walk in the park," "A visit to the beach," "Playing soccer outside"). The plan includes the date, time, and basic activities. Then, groups exchange their "Trip Plan" with another group.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The receiving group acts as "Weather Experts." They must write a "Weather Advice" response to the other group's plan. This response should:
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Include a weather prediction for the trip date (e.g., "On [date], it will be cloudy in the morning.").</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">List items to bring based on their prediction (e.g., "You should bring a light jacket and a water bottle.").</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Respond to a potential peer comment or adjust the plan with a recommendation (e.g., "It is a good idea to bring umbrellas in case it rains," or "Maybe you should change the afternoon activity if it gets windy.").</li>
          </ul>
        The task focuses on using future simple, modal verbs for recommendations, and relevant weather vocabulary in a communicative written context, directly aligning with the theme "How Will the Weather Change Tomorrow?" and scenario "Our Weather Report".</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their "Weather Advice" written responses from Stage 4. The teacher collects them for a quick formative check.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation/Checklist):</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses future simple tense for predictions (e.g., 'It will be sunny.').</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Includes relevant vocabulary (e.g., 'sunny,' 'rainy,' 'cloudy,' 'bring,' 'temperature').</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses modal verbs for recommendations (e.g., 'You should bring...').</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responds clearly to the peer plan with advice.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and comprehensibility of written sentences.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket by writing down answers to two prompts:
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I used today in my writing is: ________."</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can now write a sentence like: ________."</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of writing weather advice was easiest for me today?"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of writing weather advice was most difficult for me today, and why?"</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
          <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The structured writing tasks, especially the peer advice activity, seemed to engage students and allowed them to apply the target grammar and vocabulary. Most students were able to correctly use the future simple and recommend items. Some students needed more scaffolding with sentence structure for complex recommendations involving prepositions of place. I observed that emphasizing the intonation and pronunciation of key vocabulary during the modeling phase (even for a writing lesson) helps students to internalize the words, which indirectly supports their spelling and usage in writing. I will provide more sentence frames for the recommendation part in the next lesson if needed.</p>
          <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will synthesize information from various sources (including written forecasts and peer advice) to create a comprehensive weather report and present it as part of their 21st Century Skills Project, summarizing their understanding and applying all learned skills.</p>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>